 missing `value`) or when an operation fails (a missing target, an array index out of range, a failed
 `test`, removing the whole document, moving a value into its own child).
 
+## JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7386)
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, applies a JSON Merge Patch to `target`
+and returns the merged document.
+
+```ts
+applyMergePatch({ a: "b", c: 1 }, { a: null, d: 2 }); // { c: 1, d: 2 }
+applyMergePatch({ a: { b: 1, c: 2 } }, { a: { b: 3 } }); // { a: { b: 3, c: 2 } }
+applyMergePatch({ a: [1, 2] }, { a: [3] }); // { a: [3] }
+```
+
+An object patch merges member by member, recursively; a member set to `null` is removed. Any other
+patch (array, primitive or `null`) replaces the target, and so do arrays inside an object patch. A
+target that is not an object is treated as an empty object when the patch is an object. As the format
+dictates, a merge patch cannot set a member to `null`. The input is never mutated and the result shares
+no structure with the target or the patch. It does not throw.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test` (Node 22.18 or later, which runs the TypeScript sources directly).
